  { q: 'What role do ravens play in Zoroastrianism?', a: 'In Zoroastrian tradition and practice, corvids (ravens, crows, and vultures) play a practical and ritual role at the dakhma (Tower of Silence) — the elevated platforms on which the Zoroastrian dead were traditionally exposed for birds to consume, preventing pollution of earth, fire, and water by a human corpse. Birds consuming the corpse were thus agents of ritual purification. However, Zoroastrian texts also classify some birds as sacred to Ahura Mazda and others as associated with Ahriman (the evil principle). The classification of ravens varies across texts and periods, with some sources viewing them as impure carrion-eaters and others as functional participants in the purification process.' },
  { q: 'Are ravens in the Avesta?', a: 'Corvids appear in Zoroastrian textual tradition, though the specific identification of "raven" versus "crow" in ancient Persian texts requires care — ancient languages did not always distinguish the species we separate today. The Avesta (the primary Zoroastrian scriptural collection) includes lists of pure and impure animals; the classification of corvids as associated with corpse-eating places them in an ambiguous category. In later Pahlavi literature (Sassanid-era Zoroastrian texts, 3rd–7th century CE), birds connected with the dead and darkness are more fully categorised as associated with the dark principle Ahriman.' },
  { q: 'What do ravens symbolise in Persian folklore?', a: 'In Persian folk tradition influenced by Zoroastrian and Islamic heritage, the raven (Farsi: zaghak or kalāgh-e sia, "black crow") carries primarily negative associations: a bird of ill omen, associated with death and disaster. The separation motif is particularly associated with the ghurāb (raven/crow) in classical Persian poetry — the bird whose cry signals permanent separation and loss. The Persian expression "zabān-e zaghak" (the tongue of a raven/crow) means foul language or bad speech. These associations parallel the negative strand of raven symbolism found across the wider Islamic world, where the ghurāb also bears death-omen connotations.' },
